    {
      "id": 114984,
      "name": "mlangsdorf",
      "username": "mlangsdorf",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/m/c4cdca/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2014-08-20T21:02:22.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>I'm using a mix of Reaper, Testor, and Privateer Press paints on the washed and scrubbed but otherwise unprimed miniatures.</p><p>The figures are a little hydrophobic, so water based paints will sometimes recede from where you put the paint down. A little persistence will pay off and the paint will hold, though. It does make it hard to do some stains, so if you're heavily into washes and stains, you'll probably need to prime the figures first.</p><p>Also, some of the figures lack detail in key areas, especially the faces. I'm sure really skilled people can make them good, but they're tricky and you'll really need good lighting to make out the details.</p><p>All that said, I can't see why Citadel paints wouldn't work, and I'd rather work with a paint set that I knew (in terms of colors, thickness, etc) then get a bunch of new paints that I couldn't trust.</p><p>I put the pictures of my first set of stuff (Omnitron, golems, Bunker, AZ) here:</p><p>http://greaterthangames.com/comment/81378#comment-81378</p>",
